        <dc:subject>Read</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Useful</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>From Nile Sands to Digital Hands: Machine Translation of Coptic Texts</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The Coptic language, rooted in the historical landscapes of Egypt, continues to serve as a vital liturgical medium for the Coptic Orthodox and Catholic Churches across Egypt, North Sudan, Libya, and the United States, with approximately ten million speakers worldwide. However, the scarcity of digital resources in Coptic has resulted in its exclusion from digital systems, thereby limiting its accessibility and preservation in modern technological contexts. Our research addresses this issue by developing the most extensive parallel Coptic-centered corpus to date. This corpus comprises over 8,000 parallel sentences between Arabic and Coptic, and more than 24,000 parallel sentences between English and Coptic. We have also developed the first neural machine translation system between Coptic, English, and Arabic. Lastly, we evaluate the capability of leading proprietary Large Language Models (LLMs) to translate to and from Coptic using a few-shot learning approach (in-context learning). Our code and data are available at https://github.com/UBC-NLP/copticmt.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-08</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2024.arabicnlp-1.25/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-04 15:31:46</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:shortTitle>From Nile Sands to Digital Hands</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <bib:pages>298–308</bib:pages>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_30">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Treat this as a strategic sanity-check rather than a dataset-building playbook. It tells you that scraping real parallel text is table stakes, that you should keep your modeling setup dedicated (German↔Saxon, not general multilingual), and that you should stay open to smaller fine-tuned models even in the era of massive LLMs. For actual techniques on how to &lt;em&gt;generate&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;filter&lt;/em&gt; synthetic Saxon data, this paper offers little.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
