        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_42"/>
        <dc:subject>Read</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Useful</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Scaling Low-Resource MT via Synthetic Data Generation with LLMs</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>We investigate the potential of LLM-generated synthetic data for improving low-resource Machine Translation (MT). Focusing on seven diverse target languages, we construct a document-level synthetic corpus from English Europarl, and extend it via pivoting to 147 additional language pairs. Automatic and human evaluation confirm its overall high quality. We study its practical application by (i) identifying effective training regimes, (ii) comparing our data with the HPLT dataset, (iii) studying the effect of varying training data size, and (iiii) testing its utility beyond English-centric MT. Finally, we introduce SynOPUS, a public repository for synthetic parallel datasets. Our findings show that LLM-generated synthetic data, even when noisy, can substantially improve MT performance for low-resource languages.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025-11</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1408/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-15 11:53:50</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <bib:pages>27674–27692</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>EMNLP 2025</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_58">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;it is quite useful, but as empirical validation of your strategy rather than a direct recipe you can copy.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper demonstrates that LLM-generated synthetic parallel data can successfully bootstrap low-resource MT when aggressively filtered with automatic metrics (COMET, Bicleaner-AI, language ID, round-trip checks). This supports your core bet that you can build a usable dataset with &lt;strong&gt;no native speakers in the loop&lt;/strong&gt;. It also empirically validates two of your key downstream choices: that a &lt;strong&gt;specialized translation model&lt;/strong&gt; (like your German-centric base) will outperform a general-purpose LLM, and that when parallel data is extremely scarce, &lt;strong&gt;noisy quantity is better than nothing&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The limitation is simply that your starting point is messier: they forward-translated clean, formal English into well-behaved orthographies, whereas you must back-translate orthographically chaotic dialect text. So their pipeline proves the &lt;em&gt;concept&lt;/em&gt;, but your filtering (perplexity gating, round-trip checks, diversity controls) will need to be stricter than theirs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
