        <dc:subject>Read</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Useful</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>DIPLomA: Efficient Adaptation of Instructed LLMs to Low-Resource Languages via Post-Training Delta Merging</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>This paper investigates how open-weight instruction-tuned large language models (LLMs) can be efficiently adapted to low-resource languages without requiring costly large-scale post-training. We introduce DIPLomA (Decoupled Instruction-Preserving Language Adaptation), a lightweight delta-based transfer strategy that provides a practical and effective solution for this scenario. DIPLomA decouples language adaptation from post-training alignment by first continually pretraining a foundational LLM on a modest amount of monolingual target-language data while anchoring on English replay, and then injecting instruction-following capabilities via delta-based weight merging from the instructed counterpart of the base LLM. We evaluate DIPLomA on Basque and validate its generality on Welsh and Swahili, demonstrating consistent and substantial gains in instruction-following, linguistic proficiency, and safety. Compared to strong baselines, our method achieves average relative improvements of 50 points in Basque, 63 in Welsh, and 51 in Swahili, while preserving the original model's multilingual performance. These results highlight DIPLomA as an effective, resource-efficient strategy for bringing high-quality instruction alignment to underrepresented languages at scale.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025-11</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1355/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-15 11:53:55</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:shortTitle>DIPLomA</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <bib:pages>24898–24912</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>Findings 2025</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_60">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;This paper is useful as strong empirical validation for your planned two‑stage pipeline: it shows that continual pre‑training on monolingual low‑resource data before any task‑specific transfer genuinely builds target‑language fluency, and it confirms that LLM‑as‑a‑judge can reliably substitute for human evaluation—directly supporting your no‑native‑speaker constraint. Its mixed pre‑training strategy also implies you should blend your Saxon corpus with Standard German to preserve high‑resource competence. However, the paper does not address your current dataset‑creation bottleneck, offers no guidance on handling extreme orthographic variation or constructing filtered parallel data, and proposes delta merging rather than the LoRA versus ICL comparison you intend. Use it as evidence that hoarding monolingual Saxon text is worthwhile, but not as a guide for corpus building.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
