        <dc:subject>Read</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>LexC-Gen: Generating Data for Extremely Low-Resource Languages with Large Language Models and Bilingual Lexicons</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Data scarcity in low-resource languages can be addressed with word-to-word translations from labeled task data in high-resource languages using bilingual lexicons. However, bilingual lexicons often have limited lexical overlap with task data, which results in poor translation coverage and lexicon utilization. We propose lexicon-conditioned data generation LexC-Gen, a method that generates low-resource-language classification task data at scale. Specifically, LexC-Gen first uses high-resource-language words from bilingual lexicons to generate lexicon-compatible task data, and then it translates them into low-resource languages with bilingual lexicons via word translation. Across 17 extremely low-resource languages, LexC-Gen generated data is competitive with expert-translated gold data, and yields on average 5.6 and 8.9 points improvement over existing lexicon-based word translation methods on sentiment analysis and topic classification tasks respectively. Through ablation study, we show that conditioning on bilingual lexicons is the key component of LexC-Gen. LexC-Gen serves as a potential solution to close the performance gap between open-source multilingual models, such as BLOOMZ and Aya-101, and state-of-the-art commercial models like GPT-4o on low-resource-language tasks.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-11</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-emnlp.818/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-15 11:54:01</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:shortTitle>LexC-Gen</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <bib:pages>13990–14009</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>Findings 2024</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_62">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The paper is &lt;strong&gt;marginally useful but largely mismatched&lt;/strong&gt; to your project. LexC-Gen targets &lt;em&gt;classification&lt;/em&gt; tasks (sentiment, topics) via word-to-word lexicon substitution, whereas you need &lt;em&gt;parallel text generation&lt;/em&gt; for bidirectional dialect translation. Its word-level substitution approach assumes a fixed lexicon and preserves source-language syntax—both fatal for your goal, given Saxon’s severe orthographic variation and lack of standard spelling. Where it does help is in validating that &lt;strong&gt;LLM-generated synthetic data combined with aggressive automatic filtering&lt;/strong&gt; can approximate gold-quality corpora in extremely low-resource settings without native speakers, which loosely supports your back-translation and filtering pipeline. You should not adopt its core word-to-word method, but you can cite its evidence that quality control outperforms blind scaling when constructing synthetic parallel data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
