        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_69"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_68"/>
        <dc:subject>Read</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Useful</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Large Language Models as a Normalizer for Transliteration and Dialectal Translation</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>NLP models trained on standardized language data often struggle with variations. We assess various Large Language Models (LLMs) for transliteration and dialectal normalization. Tuning open-source LLMs with as little as 10,000 parallel examples using LoRA can achieve results comparable to or better than closed-source LLMs. We perform dialectal normalization experiments for twelve South Asian languages and dialectal translation experiments for six language continua worldwide. The dialectal normalization task can also be a preliminary step for the downstream dialectal translation task. Among the six languages used in dialectal translation, our approach enables Italian and Swiss German to surpass the baseline model by 21.5 and 25.8 BLEU points, respectively.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025-01</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2025.vardial-1.5/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-18 16:40:42</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <bib:pages>39–67</bib:pages>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_69">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;This paper is highly relevant to your project because your core task — &lt;strong&gt;Saxon ↔ Standard German translation&lt;/strong&gt; — is functionally the same problem the paper calls &lt;strong&gt;dialect normalization&lt;/strong&gt;. The paper shows that dialect-to-standard transformation can be treated as a standard seq2seq/LLM translation task and that even highly non-standard dialects with inconsistent spelling can be modeled effectively using LoRA-tuned open-source LLMs with relatively small parallel datasets (~10k examples). The Swiss German results are especially important because Swiss German shares many structural properties with Saxon: strong regional variation, phonetic spelling, weak orthographic standardization, and closeness to Standard German.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper strongly supports several of your central design decisions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
using &lt;strong&gt;sentence-level parallel pairs&lt;/strong&gt;,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
avoiding orthographic normalization,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
relying on &lt;strong&gt;subword tokenization&lt;/strong&gt;,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
prioritizing authentic dialect text,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
and adapting German-centric models with LoRA instead of training from scratch.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Its most valuable contribution for your project is probably the analysis of spelling variation and “leakage.” The authors show that weaker models often memorize dialect spellings rather than learning robust dialect mappings, which directly validates your concern about orthographic instability in Saxon. Their experiments suggest that strong LLMs generalize much better across spelling variants than smaller scratch-trained systems.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What the paper does &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; solve is your hardest problem: large-scale automatic corpus construction and filtering under extreme data scarcity. It assumes the existence of parallel dialect-standard data, whereas your project is primarily about &lt;em&gt;creating&lt;/em&gt; that data. So the paper is more valuable as evidence that your planned modeling approach is viable than as a guide for your dataset mining pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
